<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
FastDDS profile forcing UDP-only transport (no shared memory).

The SHM transport leaves lock files/segments under /dev/shm
(fastdds_port*, fastdds_<guid>, sem.fastdds_*) that don't always get
cleaned up across the frequent node restarts this robot's dev/test
workflow involves -- a stale lock file then makes a fresh participant's
SHM port bind fail (RTPS_TRANSPORT_SHM Error: Failed init_port ...),
which can silently break a subscription's discovery of a publisher on
another node entirely (no crash, no message on the failing side --
just messages that never arrive). UDP loopback is still effectively
instant for this robot's traffic (small messages at rates measured in
tens of Hz), and inter-board traffic already crosses the USB-gadget
link as UDP regardless, so there is no gain from SHM at the cost of
this reliability problem.

Wired in via FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE in each systemd unit.
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
    <profiles>
        <transport_descriptors>
            <transport_descriptor>
                <transport_id>udp_only_transport</transport_id>
                <type>UDPv4</type>
                <!-- An interfaceWhiteList pinning this to the usb0 gadget subnet
                was tried here (2026-08-09) to rule out ambiguous locator
                selection across this robot's two active interfaces (WiFi +
                usb0) as the cause of a Pi5<->Zero delivery failure. CIDR
                notation (192.168.250.0/24) is NOT valid for FastDDS's
                whitelist -- confirmed on hardware: it filtered out every
                interface ("All whitelist interfaces were filtered out"),
                breaking DATA_READER creation entirely, worse than the
                original bug. Reverted. If revisiting this, FastDDS expects
                exact interface addresses here, not a network/CIDR pattern --
                and since Pi 5 and Zero each have a DIFFERENT usb0 address
                (.2 / .1), a single shared XML file can't whitelist both by
                exact address without templating it per-board. -->
            </transport_descriptor>
        </transport_descriptors>
        <participant profile_name="udp_only_participant" is_default_profile="true">
            <rtps>
                <userTransports>
                    <transport_id>udp_only_transport</transport_id>
                </userTransports>
                <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
            </rtps>
        </participant>
    </profiles>
</dds>
